Cancellation points as POSIX has them, made as musl makes them (after
patches 0038 and 0040). mlibc's SIGCANCEL handler acted on a deferred
cancel only through BeforeCancellableSyscall, which Radix lacked, so a
deferred cancel ended a thread at pthread_testcancel alone: a thread
blocked in read, accept, sleep, a condition or a semaphore was never
cancelled. Four sysdeps are new. CancelInContext is all the handler asks
of a system layer whose cancellation points are cancellable system calls
of musl's kind: a context about to make such a call, or to make it again
after another handler ran, is sent where the call acts on the cancel once
the handler returns, so that cleanup handlers run outside the signal
handler; SIGCANCEL stays blocked there, and anywhere else it is sent
again, for a cancellation point that another handler interrupted. The
handler has SA_RESTART then, so that other calls go on as they would
without it. CancellableRead and CancellableWrite are read(2) and
write(2) as cancellation points, while stdio's and the C library's own
reads and writes are not; CancellableFutexWait is the wait of
pthread_cond_wait, sem_wait and pthread_join, which fails with ECANCELED
so that a condition's mutex is locked again before the cancel is acted
on. open, openat, close, sem_wait and clock_nanosleep are cancellation
points also when they need not wait, as on musl. system is one, with a cleanup handler of its own,
which needs no memory, that kills the command and puts the signals back,
as glibc's does; and its SIGINT and SIGQUIT are counted across threads,
as glibc's are: one thread's system that ended while another's command
ran let SIGINT reach the process, and the later one then put SIG_IGN
back for good. pclose, wordexp and closedir are no cancellation points,
as on musl, which would leave a child or a stream to nobody. A thread
that exits disables its cancellation, so that a cancellation point in a
cleanup handler or a destructor no longer ends it before the rest have
run, and its cleanup handlers run before the destructors of its
thread_local objects, which they may use, as on glibc. A program's first
thread starts with SIGCANCEL unblocked, which a thread whose
cancellation was disabled when it called exec left blocked. Meant for
upstream.

